# The adic valuation of a place is rank one discrete

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.adicValuation_isRankOneDiscrete`
(`e61cf2d8-b833-5852-a0ce-1d522102303e`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `add10091-f0c0-5cd0-9479-26a5cf193138`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the
sense of the project's structure `Place K F`: a valuation subring $\mathcal{O}_v \subseteq F$ which
contains $\operatorname{algebraMap} K F (a)$ for every $a \in K$, which is not all of $F$, and which
is a principal ideal ring. Since $\mathcal{O}_v$ is then a discrete valuation ring, its maximal
ideal is a point of the height-one spectrum, and `v.adicValuation` is the associated
$\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued valuation on $F$ obtained from that height-one prime, i.e. the normalised
valuation attached to $v$. The assertion is that this valuation satisfies
`Valuation.IsRankOneDiscrete`: it is a rank one discrete valuation, its value group being nontrivial
and generated by a single element. The statement is phrased as a theorem about an arbitrary place
rather than as an instance, so that it can be introduced locally where needed.

This records that the normalised valuation of a place of a function field $F/K$ is discrete of rank
one, the basic fact underlying the definition of the order function $\operatorname{ord}_v$ and hence
of divisors and the divisor class group. It is used in the identification of valuation subrings of a
rational function field with the points of the height-one spectrum, namely in
(`AlgebraicCurve.RationalFunctionField.toValuationSubring_eq_of_forall_ne_ofHeightOneSpectrum`).

